Instructors in higher education across the United States are faced with
a looming April deadline to update their course materials to comply with
new digital accessibility standards. Creating accessible materials
presents a significant challenge for faculty, many of whom are
unfamiliar with standards for digital accessibility.

Users of the document preparation software \LaTeX, which includes many
faculty in the \acro{STEM} disciplines of physical chemistry,
engineering, physics, and mathematics, are particularly strained by the
new standards because of the \TeX{} engine's intrinsic lack of
accessibility features. While heroic efforts have been made to make
\LaTeX \ more accessible, there is no general method that allows
straightforward remediation of existing projects, especially if the
contents of these projects may change in the future.\looseness-1

This article highlights some background of the problems surrounding
digital accessibility and suggests an indirect method for the creation
of accessible materials from \LaTeX \ source code. Some proactive advice
is also given to ensure that documents retain the author's intended
content to the greatest extent possible. This workflow is amenable to
continual updating of document contents without additional arduous
interventions. Nonetheless, sustained future efforts will be necessary
for accessibility to become practical, especially for users of \LaTeX.
